  <p class="wa-label">A note from Josh</p>
  <div class="wa-note">
    <p>Hey everyone — thank you for being Wild Atlas supporters. This is the first newsletter, and the plan is simple: keep you in the loop on what we're building, and throw in the occasional free something for the kids along the way.</p>
    <p>Version 1.0.5 went out this week, and it's the biggest update since launch — every pack you own now comes with its own printable coloring book. Also in this issue: a free printable set just for subscribers, and something for parents of little ones who aren't reading yet.</p>
    <p class="wa-note-sig">— Josh, Wild Atlas</p>
  </div>

  <p class="wa-label">What's new in 1.0.5</p>

  <div class="wa-card">
    <h3>Printables — a coloring book for every pack</h3>
    <p>Twenty pages per pack: a cover, one page for every animal, and a closing note. Not clip art — each page starts from the same animal reference the app itself uses, redrawn as line art in a real habitat, with three facts around the edge in the voice the app narrates in. Find it at Home → About → Printables, behind the parental gate.</p>
    <p><a class="wa-btn" href="/blog/printable-coloring-books/">See what a page looks like</a></p>
  </div>

  <h3>Also in this update</h3>
  <ul class="wa-list">
    <li><strong>Packs update themselves.</strong> New content for a pack you own downloads quietly in the background over Wi-Fi — no manual refresh before your kid can play.</li>
    <li><strong>Setup is a straight line.</strong> First-run is now one step at a time: name, avatar, and an explicit consent step you can't decline by accident.</li>
    <li><strong>Gift codes tell you where to go.</strong> If someone sends you a code for a pack, the Shop now points you straight at the screen to redeem it.</li>
    <li><strong>Anonymous usage statistics.</strong> A new off-by-default toggle in the Grown-ups area. Counts only — which packs get opened, whether a download failed. No names, no identifiers, never third parties. If you're willing to turn it on, it genuinely helps us see what's working.</li>
  </ul>

  <hr class="wa-divider">

  <p class="wa-label">Free for subscribers</p>
  <div class="wa-gift">
    <p class="wa-gift-label">Print &amp; hand over</p>
    <h3>A coloring set you won't find in the app</h3>
    <p>The books inside Wild Atlas follow the packs you own. This one doesn't — we made it for issue one only. The Saola. The Pangolin. The Axolotl. Download, print, and hand it over — no setup required.</p>
    <p style="margin-bottom:0;"><a class="wa-btn" href="/assets/downloads/wild-atlas-coloring-book-issue-01.pdf">Download free PDF</a></p>
  </div>

  <hr class="wa-divider">

  <p class="wa-label">Child development &middot; Learning</p>
  <h2>Before They Can Read: How Wild Atlas Works for Pre-Readers</h2>
  <p>Most children's apps designed for young kids still require a grown-up nearby. Wild Atlas was built to make that unnecessary.</p>
  <p>Every fact is narrated. Every quiz question is read aloud. A three-year-old who cannot identify a single letter can open Wild Atlas, tap an animal, and hear everything — from start to finish, no reading required. Here's why that was the whole point.</p>
  <p><a class="wa-btn" href="/blog/wild-atlas-for-pre-readers/">Read the full piece</a></p>

  <hr class="wa-divider">

  <p class="wa-label">New pack</p>
  <h2>Legends of the Wild</h2>
  <p>Eighteen of the rarest animals on Earth — the Amur Tiger, the West Indian Manatee, and sixteen more — narrated like everything else in the app.</p>
  <p>And the Saola: first documented by scientists in 1992, so rarely encountered that researchers have never observed one in the wild. Fewer than a hundred are thought to exist, making it one of the rarest large mammals alive.</p>
  <p><a class="wa-btn" href="/blog/legends-of-the-wild/">Meet the Legends</a></p>

